  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nIn the case where a history traversal navigation is intercepted, #resume-applying-the-traverse-history-step is expected to be called asynchronously, but no implementation does this entirely. [WebKit](https://searchfox.org/wubkat/source/Source/WebCore/page/Navigation.cpp#1058,1070,1073-1074) performs synchronous steps, as well as [Chromium](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/navigation_api/navigation_api.cc;l=885), and [Gecko](https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D269220#inline-1471736) is in the process of adding synchronous steps.\n\nTests concerned are, amongst others. [navigation-api/navigate-event/intercept-same-document-history-back.html](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/source/testing/web-platform/tests/navigation-api/navigate-event/intercept-same-document-history-back.html), but also tests listed in [Bug 1992939](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1992939). This bug reflects that for Firefox this broke Reddit, which prompted us to do this spec breaking change.\n\nThe root cause was that the current navigation entry needs to be updated before event handlers run.",
